## Phase 3. API keys

Copy `env.local.template` to `.env.local` and fill it in.

12. **Notion** (the blog). Needs `NOTION_API_KEY` and `NOTION_DATABASE_ID`. `NOTION_DATA_SOURCE_ID` is
    optional, it resolves itself. The database needs these properties: Title or Name (title), Slug (rich
    text), Tags (multi-select), Published (checkbox), Published Date or Date (date), Description (rich
    text). Remember to share the database with the integration in Notion, otherwise the key alone does
    nothing. Once this is live the three fake posts disappear from the home page, /writing, the post
    pages, and the sitemap.

13. **Photos** (the home carousel). Needs `PHOTOS_BASE_URL` plus `R2_ACCOUNT_ID`, `R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`,
    `R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, `R2_BUCKET`. Then drop your photos into a `photos-src/` folder and run
    `npm run photos:sync`. Filenames sort into display order, so prefix them 010-, 020-, and so on.
    Optional `photos-src/meta.json` adds alt text and captions.

14. **Set the same values in Vercel, then trigger a fresh deploy.** This matters more than it sounds.
    See the trap below.

### The trap that will bite you

`next.config.ts` (line 20) reads `PHOTOS_BASE_URL` when the Next config is *loaded*, which is build time,
to allowlist the image host. If you add that variable in Vercel after a build has already happened, the
photos will still load their manifest, the sample chip will vanish, and every single image will fail with
a 400. It looks like the carousel is broken. It isn't. You just need to redeploy so the config picks up
the variable.

Two smaller things in the same file, worth fixing while you're there:

- A value without `https://` in front of it (just `photos.sagdi.com`) throws "Invalid URL" and kills the
  build outright. An empty value is safe, it just falls back to samples.
- The protocol is hardcoded to `https`, so an `http://` host can never be allowlisted. Fine for R2,
  annoying if you ever want a local mock.

